#### A surgeon, a committee I never met, and a blood pressure spike the day after that made the whole conversation feel a lot less theoretical.

> Originally published on Substack. Imported to My Ledger as part of the adrenal journey series.

If you're just finding this series, [Chapter 1](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-1/) has the full story of how I got here. The short of it: seventeen years of "unrelated" diagnoses. High blood pressure, type 2 diabetes, cataracts decades early, a body that ran hot and cold at the wrong times. They turned out to share one cause nobody ever stepped back far enough to look for. I kept pushing; that led to imaging, which found something concrete: masses on both of my adrenal glands. That's where Chapter 1 ends.

On August 14th, I sat down with an endocrine surgeon at UT Southwestern in Dallas to talk about those two masses. It wasn't a long appointment. It was long enough.

The options were about what I expected, once I'd read enough to know what to expect. First, adrenal vein sampling, or AVS, to find out if one gland is overproducing cortisol on its own. If it is, deal with that one and see if the other one wakes back up. If it's not that clean, there's medication to blunt the impact of the extra cortisol. And if none of that works, there's the option nobody wants to say out loud first: take both glands out.

A fair number of my questions were sent elsewhere: "that's really a question for your medical endocrinologist or PCP."  When I brought up some frustrations with my current endocrinologist, she politely shut it down. I get it. She wasn't going to step outside her lane or take a swing at another doctor's judgment, and I'd rather have a surgeon with that kind of discipline than one without it.

Here's what surprised me. She said that later that same afternoon, UTSW was holding an adrenal multidisciplinary conference. Endocrinologists, endocrine surgeons, and endocrine radiologists in one room, going through cases together. Mine would be one of them. I left that appointment happier than I expected. After seventeen years of doctors each treating one piece of me at a time, a room full of specialists looking at the whole picture together was the first thing in this whole process that felt like the system working the way I'd always wished it would.

That evening I got a message from the surgeon. The committee had made its recommendation. Here's the note, word for word:

> **Endocrine Surgery Staff — Adrenal Multi Disciplinary Conference discussion**
> 
> Case reviewed today with medical endocrinology, radiology and surgery. Labs reviewed. Agree with diagnosis of hypercortisolism. Imaging reviewed. Agree with bilateral discrete adrenal nodules, however the right side has a lower non contrast HU than the left. This may suggest different potential benign etiologies.
> 
> Recommendation from group was to proceed with adrenal vein sampling with dexamethasone suppression. Will place IR referral.
> 
> Recommendation was in agreement with med endo assessment here at UTSW, as well as consideration of trial of medical therapy after AVS to clarify the subjective symptoms as caused by cortisol, vs surgery as debulking option.

In plain English: do the sampling first, figure out which gland is the real problem, and maybe try medication before anybody commits to surgery. I was relieved. Taking both glands out, a bilateral adrenalectomy, was still on the table, and that's not a small thing to live with. I did make one thing clear to the team. If the medication comes with known liver toxicity, I'd rather have the surgery. I'm not adding liver disease on top of everything else already stacked up. I'd rather lose the glands than pick up a second problem while trying to fix the first.

I also left with a referral to a medical endocrinologist at UTSW. Apparently, the only way in the door with one there is a referral like this. You can't just call and ask.

Five days later, on August 19th, I met her. The appointment was an hour long, the longest I've had with any doctor in this whole process, and no prescription pad came out in the first five minutes. She took a full history and asked real questions. By the last fifteen minutes, I could tell she was watching the clock. That's a fair trade for the forty-five minutes before it.

Somewhere in that hour she mentioned she'd been in the room for the August 14th conference. She was one of the specialists who reviewed my case and voted on the recommendation before I ever sat down in front of her. That was a strange feeling, being met by somebody who had already seen me, in a way, before she ever met me. I'm still deciding how I feel about it. Mostly I'm grateful. It meant my first real conversation with my new endocrinologist didn't start from zero. And "the committee" I mentioned at the end of Chapter 1 wasn't some faceless panel. At least one of them was about to be my doctor.

We talked about the liver toxicity question head-on. She confirmed there are medication options without that risk, which was good to hear. She also told me something I hadn't really let sink in yet. These tumors have very likely been there for years, possibly decades, growing the whole time slowly and causing exactly the kind of scattered damage I wrote about in Chapter 1. Seventeen years of "unrelated" problems, potentially explained by two things sitting quietly on my adrenal glands the whole time. She didn't want to get into what life after a bilateral adrenalectomy would look like. Not yet. Her thinking: do the AVS first, see what it shows, and don't burn energy worrying about something that might never happen. I appreciated that. I try to live the same way. It's just harder when it's your own body on the table.

Here's where it ties straight back to Chapter 1. When they took my blood pressure at the start of that appointment, it was 154 over 93. That's high, and it's part of a trend that's been climbing for a while, even on medication, along with shortness of breath and a heart rate that won't behave. Nobody checked it again before I left, and I forgot to bring it back up as the appointment wrapped up. One more small thing that fell through one more small gap. The system is full of them.

The next day, that gap got real. Around 2:30 in the afternoon, my heart rate started climbing for no reason I could point to. No exercise, no stress I knew of, nothing. At 4:29 p.m. I checked: **blood pressure 152/102, heart rate 112 beats per minute.** It stayed up for about six hours, peaking at 127 beats per minute at one point, before it finally settled back to 127 over 86 around 8:30 that night. Six hours is a long time for your body to do something you didn't ask it to do and can't explain.

My new endocrinologist messaged me the next morning with real guidance, not a shrug. If most of my readings keep running above 140 systolic, we adjust the blood pressure medication. Below that, keep monitoring. **Above 180 systolic, that's not a monitor-it situation anymore. That's go and get evaluated now.**

I have an appointment with my primary care doctor next week. I haven't needed to see her in two years, because the cardiologist has been handling blood pressure and cholesterol, and the endocrinologist has been handling, well, everything else. Including things that turned out not to be "everything else" at all. Before that appointment, I've sent her every updated lab result. I've granted her access to my two main providers' charts so they can see each other's notes without me having to carry them back and forth. And I've given her an updated list of every medication and supplement I take, with the schedule. Nobody gave me that job. I took it, because I already know what happens when nobody has it.

Chapter 3 is when the AVS results come back. Until then, I'm watching the numbers on a blood pressure cuff closer than I ever expected to, and trusting a committee I've never sat in a room with to get this right.
